<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mandala (political model)</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as galactic polity</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The mandala political model is a scholarly concept describing states in pre-modern South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> as circles of power radiating from a central ruler. Authority weakened with distance, and outlying rulers shifted allegiance among centres. The concept is used to explain how <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a> held together a loose network of ports and tributaries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mandala-political-model.jpg" alt="Mandala (political model)" width="320" height="315" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gunkarta · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Southeast_Asian_Historical_Mandalas.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Srivijaya's government has been described by scholars as a '<b>galactic polity</b>' or mandala: a centre-periphery arrangement in which the paramount ruler at Palembang exercised suzerainty over subordinate port lords who kept substantial autonomy within a framework of ritual submission, commercial obligation and periodic tribute.</blockquote><a href="/empire/srivijaya-empire">Srivijaya Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
